    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:18:2",
      "id": "thm-morse-functions-are-dense-by-relative-jet-transversality",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 defines B_n=K_n\\(W\\cup int K_{n-1}) but requires every correction to have support in (K_{n+1}\\setminus K_{n-1})\\setminus W, then claims its parameter derivatives span every cotangent fibre over B_n. Points of \\partial K_{n-1}\\setminus W belong to B_n, while every such supported coordinate bump vanishes on a neighbourhood of K_{n-1}, so its differential is zero there and cannot span a positive-dimensional cotangent fibre. For example take M=R, A=V=W=empty, h(x)=x^2, and n=2: x=1 lies in B_2 and no permitted bump has nonzero differential at x=1.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b1bd38d9ba01687109603d856a3965afbc288608db7e86c166aa32031205ae8b"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:18:3",
      "id": "prop-proper-morse-exhaustions-exist-on-smooth-manifolds",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The cited relative-density theorem guarantees only that g-f is supported in M\\setminus A and that dg is transverse everywhere. It does not guarantee that its perturbation is supported in a prescribed compact shell K_{n+1}\\setminus K_{n-1}. Nevertheless step 2.1 says to \"choose it small enough\" so that the correction has that shell support. Smallness cannot impose support, and no compactly supported relative transversality result is cited or proved; the shell decomposition used in step 3.1 is therefore unsupported.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e791f1396d5cdeb658f45abfe6d839af00dbe93bd56c8776d637040eee5749b1"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:18:4",
      "id": "rem-noncompact-morse-genericity-is-not-an-open-dense-claim",
      "location": "title",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The title and Remark say that in the strong topology one should say \"residual,\" not \"globally open dense,\" unless extra hypotheses are imposed. This is false for the strong Whitney topology used here: the zero section of T*M is a closed embedded submanifold, and sections transverse to a closed target form an open as well as dense set in the strong topology. The opened dependency fs-transversality-is-globally-open-for-arbitrary-noncompact-sources-in-the-uniform-c1-topology only refutes openness for a single uniform C^1 bound; it does not refute strong-topology openness.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "353a78d23bfa9a6d8e7a8c35e5ccf3f6d3b097cade7d3310e58d4797f43a303e"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:18:5",
      "id": "thm-excellent-morse-functions-are-open-dense-on-a-compact-manifold",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The C^infty-density asserted in the Statement and concluded in 3.1 is not obtained from the cited L2. Its exact Statement supplies only a perturbation satisfying ||g-f||_{C^2}<epsilon. A C^infty neighbourhood may impose a bound on derivatives of order greater than 2, so C^2-smallness does not ensure the separating perturbation remains in that neighbourhood. Step 1.1 supplies no higher-order estimate, and the appeal in 3.1 to L1 does not repair this gap after the Morse function is separated.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "fd10ec7dd3e43009448dd5ac44f410efabd159dd971fb93c5d148aae4b39cbfb"
    }
  ],
